/**
\page Features

!!! Supported Platforms and Toolsets
\n
Most modules of the Platinum C++ framework can be compiled for any platform
that provides an ISO C++ implementation, the system dependent parts of %Pt 
are designed to be very portable. The %Pt build process supports a variety
of platforms and compilers. The following table shows the exact possible
combinations:

||#Platform#||#Toolset#
|Windows 2000, NT, XP|VC7, VC8, MinGW
|Windows Mobile|VC8, (MinGW)
|Linux/Unix|GCC 3.x, GCC 4.x
|QNX 6.3.x|QCC 3.3.x

Big-endian as well as little-endian architectures are supported 
including X86, PPC, ARM, SHA. 64-bit support is available for 
the respective platforms.


!!! Features
\n
!!Modular Design
Platinum is highly modular and every module builds to a separate libray. 
Library modules can depend on other modules and form a hierarchy. The 
core module is relatively small and the extentions make up for the 
most part of the library. This allows the programmer to select only 
the parts of the framework that he really needs.

!!Consistent use of the C++ Standard Library
The C++ standard library is available for almost all devices. It is one
of the best documented pieces of software in the C++ world and a great
example how both, good design and high performance can be achieved. %Pt
truly embraces the C++ standard library like few others do and benefits
greatly from the effort the designers have put into it. IO is done 
with real iostreams and there is no need for yet another string class or
set of container classes. All exceptions thrown by %Pt are derived from 
std::exception and std::allocators can be used when needed.

!!Error Handling using Exceptions
In traditional C/C++ programs without exceptions approximately half of
the code is required for error handling. Error states constantly have to
be checked and passed through the many layers that form a big application.
This is not only troublesome and error-prone, but can also have a very negative
effect on the performance. It requires to check for errors even in situations
when no error has occured. Exceptions have been introduced to the C++ 
language to solve these problems. They effectively separate error handling
from the normal program flow, which lets the program run faster and eliminates
many lines of code.

!!Generic Programming
Platinum makes extensive use of templates and generic programming and this is the 
%main reason why it beats most other toolkits in performance. This technique is
sometimes refered to as compile time polymorphism, because a compiler is able
to inline code more often and this eliminate many function calls. But 
performance is is not the only reason for templates. Generic programming
offers flexibility and code reuse that can not easily achieved with classic
object-oriented methods.

!!Object-Orientation
Most of the framework consists of classes - it is a class library after all!
Multiple inheritance and RTTI are supported, and there are some prime example
within %Pt how these often misunderstood features can lead to elegant and 
robust code.

!!Namespaces
Platinum opens the namespace %Pt, which spans over all
provided modules. All modules open their own module-wide namespace i.e.
The Pt-IO module uses the %Pt::IO namespace. All macros are given a prefix
derived from the namespace of the module in which they are defined.
The macro prefix equals the namespace in upper case with the namespace
operator replaced by underscores. Macros from the Pt::IO module are
prefixed with PT_IO_.

- #The core module#
The core module provides basic types and support for object modelling. A template based 'signal/slot mechanism' and object reflection is available.

- #The I/O module#
The I/O module extends standard C++ IOStreams and allows transparent and asynchronous I/O.

- #The System module#
The system module provides classes for multi-threading and concurrency, file and directory management, file I/O, library loading and plugins.

- #The Net module#
The networking module provides TCP and UDP I/O over various address spaces Network I/O is available through standard C++ IOStreams.

- #The Text module#
The text handling module implements Unicode support and classes to encode and decode between various encodings.

- #The Xml module#
The Xml module provides an Xml pull parser and an Xml writer.

- #The Gfx module#
The graphics module offers support for graphics operations and drawing on images in many different color spaces (ARGB, CMYK, YUV, HSV) and for conversion between them. It extends the I/O module to allow reading and writing to various popular image formats (.jpg, .png)

- #The Gui module#
The Gui module provides basic widget classes, an API for event handling and extends the drawing API of the Gfx module to allow drawing on widgets and widget back buffers.
*/

